#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# drop_failures

#% text
# `drop_failures(xs)` gives the elements of the list `xs` that are not failures and contain no failure.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# An element that holds a failure anywhere inside it, such as a row `[x, failure(...)]`, is dropped whole.

#% item
# The other elements keep their order.

#% item
# Use it after a computation over a grid where some points have no result, before interpolating or plotting.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
drop_failures([1, failure(no_root, find_root), 3])
#% output
#= [1, 3]

#% cell
drop_failures([[1, 2.5], [2, failure(not_converged, n_integrate)], [3, 4.0]])
#% output
#= [[1, 2.5], [3, 4.0]]

#% cell
drop_failures([])
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A table over a grid where some points fail, cleaned before interpolation, as the wall-profile script does with its shooting results:

#% cell
fn root_near(a) = find_root(x^2 - a, [x, 1, 0, 3])
tab = table([a, x /. root_near(a)], a, [1, 4, 16, 2])
drop_failures(tab)
#% output
#= [[1, 1.0], [4, 2.0], [2, 1.4142135623730951]]

#% text
# How many points were lost:

#% cell
length(tab) - length(drop_failures(tab))
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A list without failures is unchanged:

#% cell
drop_failures([1, [2, 3], x]) === [1, [2, 3], x]
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `is_failure` tests a single value; it is false for a list that only contains a failure, which `drop_failures` still drops:

#% cell
xs = [1, failure(a, f), [2, failure(b, g)], 3]
map(is_failure, xs)
#% output
#= [false, true, false, false]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `is_failure`, `failure_tag`, `select`, `interpolation`
